        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.186,
            0.071,
            0.494,
            0.089
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Manjari | Grade 9 | Part II"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.11,
            0.881,
            0.133
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "13.5.1 Algebraic Methods for Solving a Pair of Linear Equations"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.135,
            0.152,
            0.399,
            0.175
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Think and Reflect"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.132,
            0.189,
            0.602,
            0.248
          ],
          "content": "Solve the puzzle and discuss your strategy with your friends. Can you determine the value represented by each shape?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.639,
            0.187,
            0.86,
            0.338
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_21_image_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.353,
            0.881,
            0.451
          ],
          "content": "We note from the first row that the values of 2 triangles and 2 rectangles add up to 28. We may express this as $2T + 2R = 28$ or as $\\mathrm{T} + \\mathrm{R} = 14$ . Similarly, the last column may be written as $2C + T + R = 22$ Here T, S and C represent the values assigned to a triangle, a rectangle and a circle respectively."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.455,
            0.881,
            0.514
          ],
          "content": "Since we know that T + R = 14, we can rewrite the second equation as $2C + 14 = 22$ , solving which, we get $\\mathbb { C } \\equiv 4 .$ . Once you have the value of C, you can find the values of the other shapes. Try it for yourself."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.517,
            0.882,
            0.576
          ],
          "content": "We will now learn two methods for solving a pair of linear equations. One is called the **method of substitution** while the other is referred to as the **method of elimination**."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.581,
            0.881,
            0.621
          ],
          "content": "**Substitution Method:** We will demonstrate the method of substitution by solving a pair of linear equations in two variables."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.625,
            0.881,
            0.664
          ],
          "content": "**Example 7:** Solve the following pair of equations by substitution method:"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.418,
            0.664,
            0.879,
            0.683
          ],
          "content": "7x - 15y = 2\\tag{1}",
          "image_source": "images/page_21_equation_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.442,
            0.69,
            0.879,
            0.71
          ],
          "content": "x + 2y = 3\\tag{2}",
          "image_source": "images/page_21_equation_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.71,
            0.264,
            0.729
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Solution:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.736,
            0.881,
            0.773
          ],
          "content": "**Step 1:** We select either of the equations and express one variable in terms of the other."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.774,
            0.805,
            0.793
          ],
          "content": "Let us consider Equation (2), that is, x + 2y = 3 and write it as"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.43,
            0.793,
            0.879,
            0.812
          ],
          "content": "x = 3 - 2 y\\tag{3}",
          "image_source": "images/page_21_equation_16.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.818,
            0.881,
            0.877
          ],
          "content": "**Step 2:** Clearly, all $( x , y )$ that satisfy both the given equations must also satisfy $x = 3 - 2 y$ . Since this relation must hold true in Equation (1), substituting $x = 3 -$ 2y in it we get"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.403,
            0.881,
            0.597,
            0.901
          ],
          "content": "7(3 - 2y) - 15y = 2",
          "image_source": "images/page_21_equation_18.jpg"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
